# The compiler of language 4, a program of the chain (languages 1-3): the pass over a text of language 3 in which `copy` may have the address of the host
# (a number 0x1 and 32 hex digits: 2^128 + the position area * 2^96 + bit). stdin: that text and a zero byte; stdout: the text of language 3 and a zero byte
# (what `l3c` reads). The chain of the build is
#     text of language 4 -> l4c -> l3c -> [l3p] -> l2l -> seed -> the image
# Language 4 has no word of its own: the pass gives the text as it is (tokens as l3c reads them: blanks, `#` comments, strings with escapes) and makes of
# `copy &x 0x...` (get) and `copy 0x... &x` (put) copies of language 3, the copy with the address of a channel (the number 1048570, S) among them; the prelude (the
# buffer of the request l4q of 204 + 2048 bits, the zeros l4z, the constants of the nibbles l4n0..l4n3) is put at the start (after the word `pack`, if it stands first).
# The width n of the data is that of the part `&x[lo:hi]` (hi - lo), `&x[k]` (1) or of `&x` whole: the pass remembers the widths of `:name data n ...` (the table of
# names: a tree of tables by the hash of the name, 25.3.1). A copy with no address of the host, or with two, or with a width which is not known or not 1..2048, goes out unchanged (l3c reports it). Not a word of the
# text, not an address: it goes through as it is (but the blanks between `copy` and its operands do not go out: the copy goes out as `copy a b`). No errors. The reference in Python is tower/l3ctext.py (l4c, host_copy); this text does the same on the chain and a
# test compares the bytes. How it reads: a character at a time (key); a token is held (the array tk, the two operands of a copy in its two halves) and goes out when
# it ends; `copy` is held with its two operands. The tables between the marks are made by tower/l3ctext.py (x_tables_text).

